# heic NEON vs forced-scalar — first tier-isolated measurement
date:    2026-07-28
host:    Apple M4 Pro (12 core), macOS, aarch64
command: cargo bench --bench tier_isolation --features backend-rust,_dev
build:   release, NO -C target-cpu=native
backend: backend-rust (the pure-Rust HEVC decoder — the one carrying the SIMD)
corpus:  codec-corpus/heic-conformance/valid, largest decodable files

## Result — essentially FLAT

| file        | NEON      | scalar    | speedup |
|-------------|-----------|-----------|---------|
| image2.heic | 325.09ms  | 326.61ms  |  1.005x |
| C012.heic   |  23.352ms |  23.555ms |  1.009x |

Under 1% either way. NEON is contributing almost nothing to end-to-end HEIC
decode on this host.

## Reading this correctly

This is NOT a regression and probably NOT a defect. HEVC decode is dominated by
CABAC entropy decoding, which is inherently serial (each bin's context depends
on the previous one) and cannot be vectorised. The SIMD kernels this crate does
have — 4 files under src/ mentioning neon — sit in the pixel stages
(prediction / transform / deblock) downstream of that, so even a large speedup
there moves the total very little. A ~1% end-to-end result is the expected
shape for an entropy-bound codec, not evidence of broken dispatch.

What it DOES mean: further NEON work on this crate's pixel kernels has a very
low ceiling. Amdahl's law caps it at whatever fraction of decode is not CABAC,
and these numbers bound that fraction at a few percent for these inputs. If
HEIC decode speed matters, the lever is the entropy decoder, not more SIMD.

NOT MEASURED here: the per-stage split. Confirming CABAC dominance would need a
profile; the claim above is the standard explanation for this shape, and is
offered as the likely cause, not as something measured on this run.

## Bench portability note

benches/decode.rs hardcodes `/home/lilith/work/heic` (a Linux home path) as its
default test-image root, so it silently benchmarks nothing on any other machine.
This bench instead discovers files from the shared codec-corpus conformance set
via a repo-relative path, honours HEIC_CORPUS_DIR, and skips loudly when the
corpus is missing.
